course: english
lesson: 20
title: Vocabulary in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. When you meet a new word, read the words around it: they are its context, and they leave clues. «The children were famished after the long walk.» After a long walk you are hungry, so famished must mean very hungry. That is exactly what it means.
explain: «The path was treacherous, full of loose stones and steep drops.» Leo guesses treacherous means pretty, but the clues are loose stones and steep drops, so it must mean dangerous. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Words also carry feelings. «He gave a curt reply and walked away.» Curt means short, but the walking away tells you it was short in a rude, unfriendly way. «The dog's tail drooped» is not just about a tail moving down: it shows the dog is sad or tired. Glum, sad, miserable and heartbroken all mean unhappy, but each a different amount.
explain: Words from other places work the same way. Grandad in Glasgow says, «Dinnae worry, dreich means a grey, wet day» (dinnae is Scots for don't). An Aussie mum says, «The servo's closed, love», and Ella in London has to work it out: a servo is a petrol station. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Context clues can help you work out the meaning of an unknown word.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: «Famished» means very hungry.
fact: «Treacherous» means dangerous, especially when it looks safe.
fact: «Ancient» means very old.
fact: «Curt» means short and rude.
fact: «Barely» means only just, as in «barely a whisper»: very quiet.
fact: «Drooped» means hung down, and it often shows sadness or tiredness.
fact: «Luminous» means giving out light, or glowing.
fact: Synonyms have similar meanings, but often different strength or feeling: glum is a quiet, gloomy sort of sad, miserable is very unhappy, and heartbroken is the saddest of all.
fact: You can understand a text well without knowing every word; skip, read on, then come back.
fact: Regional words: «dreich» (Scotland) means grey and wet; «dinnae» (Scotland) means don't; «servo» (Australia) means petrol station; «mid» (Gen Alpha slang) means average.
exercise: «The children were famished after the long walk.» What does famished mean? | Very hungry | Very happy to be home after the long walk | Lost in the woods | Wet through from rain
exercise: «The path was treacherous, full of loose stones and steep drops.» Leo says treacherous means pretty. What does it mean? | Dangerous | Pretty, with lovely views over the hills and the sea | Short and narrow | Muddy and slippery
exercise: «The ancient castle stood on the hill.» Find and copy one word that means very old. | ancient | castle | stood | hill
exercise: Grandad says «dreich». What does it mean? | A grey, wet day | A hot, sunny day at the seaside | A wee dog, like a terrier | A big Scottish dinner
exercise: «He gave a curt reply and walked away.» What does curt suggest? | Short and unfriendly | Long, kind and very helpful | Funny | Whispered
exercise: Nadia says «glum» is mid, just say «sad». Why might an author choose glum? | It means quiet and gloomy, a shade of sad | It means exactly the same as sad, so there is no reason at all to pick it | It means happy | It is a longer word
exercise: «Her voice was barely a whisper.» What does barely mean here? | Only just | Very loudly | Without clothes, like the word bare that sounds the same | Slowly and softly
exercise: Jayden asks, «Do I need to know every word to understand?» What is the answer? | No, context helps, and you can skip and come back | Yes, you must stop at every word you don't know and look each one up first | Yes, or the text makes no sense | No, words don't matter
exercise: «The dog's tail drooped.» What does drooped show? | The dog was sad or tired | The dog was excited and about to play a game in the garden | The dog was angry | The dog was asleep
exercise: Ella in London asks, «What's a servo?» What is it? | A petrol station | A waiter | A type of food | A school
exercise: A Year 5 class uses a dictionary to check «luminous». What does it mean? | Giving out light | Very heavy | Covered in fluffy fur, like a big friendly cat | Very old
exercise: What is context? | The words around a word | The cover of the book | The last page of a dictionary | The name of the author
exercise: What is a good way to check your guess about a word? | Put your meaning in the sentence and see if it fits | Ask someone to tell you | Write the word ten times | Skip the whole page and the next one too
exercise: Which word means the same as «ancient»? | Very old | New | Tall | Broken
exercise: «Dinnae worry,» says Grandad. What does dinnae mean? | Don't | Dinner | Do | Never
test: «famished means full up, deffo». What context clue proves this wrong? | After a long walk you'd be hungry, not full | The word starts with F | Famished is a word for a colour that is used in old books | Nobody walks that far
test: ★★★ «Maya's luminous watch was the only thing they could see in the dark cave.» What does luminous mean, and which clue shows it? | Glowing, because it could be seen in the dark | Expensive, because it was the only one | Broken, because they were in a cave | Big, because it could be seen
test: Leo: «treacherous means pretty, cos paths are pretty». What is the problem? | He ignored the clues about loose stones and steep drops | He spelt it wrong | Paths can't be pretty | Nothing, his meaning works perfectly well in the sentence
test: ★★★ «The puppy was not famished, but he was certainly peckish.» Which is closest to the meaning of peckish? | A little hungry | Very, very hungry | Not hungry at all | Thirsty
test: ★★★ Put these from least to most unhappy: heartbroken, glum, miserable. | glum, miserable, heartbroken | heartbroken, glum, miserable | miserable, glum, heartbroken | glum, heartbroken, miserable
test: In a cartoon, a teacher says «The road is treacherous» and the kids think it's a road that tells lies. What is true? | Here treacherous means dangerous | Treacherous only means telling lies | Roads can lie | Treacherous means bumpy and fun
test: «the servo's shut, we'll have to push the ute» (ute is Aussie for pickup truck). Why push it? | They probably need petrol and the petrol station is closed | The ute is too clean | A servo is a broken engine, so the ute won't start without it | They want exercise
test: Mia's mum: «Your nan's been curt with me all day.» How has Nan been talking? | Short and unfriendly | Kind and chatty | Loud and happy | In a funny accent
test: What would happen if you stopped reading every time you met a new word? | Reading would be slow and you'd lose the story | You'd read much faster | You'd never meet new words again | The words would get easier on their own
test: «barely means with no clothes on, like bare, innit». Is it? | No, barely means only just | Yes, barely and bare always mean the same | Yes, and the voice had no clothes | No, it means very loudly
exam: Identify the principal means by which a reader may determine the meaning of an unfamiliar word without recourse to a dictionary. | Consideration of the context in which it appears | Examination of the book's cover | Reference to the length of the word alone, longer words generally denoting larger things | Omission of the sentence altogether
exam: It is stated that the path was «treacherous, full of loose stones and steep drops». Select the response that most accurately characterises «treacherous». | Hazardous | Picturesque and pleasing to the eye of any traveller passing along it | Brief in length | Level and even
exam: Ascertain the meaning of «famished» as employed in the sentence «The children were famished after the long walk». | Extremely hungry | Exhausted and in need of a lengthy period of sleep before supper | Joyful and excited | Lost upon the way
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning synonyms? | Synonyms share a similar meaning but may differ in strength or feeling | Synonyms are identical in every respect | Synonyms are words that sound alike | Synonyms are words of opposite meaning, as are hot and cold or big and small
